WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.000
- We're here at Adobe's
headquarters in downtown San Jose.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.370
And this gray scale building behind me

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.070
might not be what you'd expect
from the makers of Photo Shop

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.647
but let's check out the inside.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.207
(percussive music)

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.020
Adobe built its San Jose HQ in 1994.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.670
After 20 years, it came
time for a redesign.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.470
Designer Natalie Engels
said it was in need

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.223
of a more colorful update.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.100
- You know when we first
ventured to the space,

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.120
it is exactly what you would imagine,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.014
from the outside.

00:00:50.014 --> 00:00:52.280
It was very bank, institutional like.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.430
And that's not what tech is.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.973
That's specifically not what Adobe is.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.280
- So when I walk in here as
an employee during the morning

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.004
and the first thing I see is
this vibrant orange color,

00:01:06.004 --> 00:01:07.870
what is that doing for me?

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:12.500
- It should have the right
amount of yellow in it actually

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.220
to have this happiness, a vibrancy,

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.940
but not too vibrant to agitate you.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.006
And so we really wanted,
especially after people

00:01:22.006 --> 00:01:26.320
are stuck in traffic, to
come in here and just feel

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.123
refreshed and vibrant and
ready to start their day.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.170
- [Adam] Color is more
than aesthetics at Adobe,

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:34.310
it's their business.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.410
So Engels and Adobe used color theory

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.680
throughout the space to
think more scientifically

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.300
about how hues and brightness
levels inspire work.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.900
- So when you think about it
from a scientific perspective

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.620
that orange, that's very much kind of

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.270
an invitation for community.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.920
What we find is, we find
people working down here

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.630
on their own, they're opting in.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.940
It's a place that they want to be.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.993
It's be active since we've opened it.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.680
- [Adam] Beyond fresh
color, Engels designed

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.960
a variety of spaces to accommodate
different types of work.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.510
Before the redesign, Adobe
had only closed office spaces.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.480
The focus then was on giving engineers

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.700
the quiet space they needed for work.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:13.360
Floors looked like this one.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.920
- As work has shifted, as
generations have changed,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.920
it became pretty obvious that

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.880
they needed to be together.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.200
- [Adam] So that meant opening offices up.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.770
But Adobe isn't a full
open office environment.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.413
- We really want to create
balanced work space.

00:02:29.413 --> 00:02:31.740
Do you have the right meeting rooms?

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.420
Is there a place where
you can make phone calls

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.930
where you're not disturbing others?

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:38.860
A work station or a desk is
actually about task work.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.440
- That's why each floor also has

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.550
spaces for group and
individual focused work.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.560
Adobe has these phone
booths that are dedicated

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.890
to special moments in
Photo Shop's history.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.942
This is 1990, the year
Photo Shop was invented.

00:02:50.942 --> 00:02:53.520
And they actually have the first version

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.050
installed on this Mac right here.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.020
Let's check it out.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.030
Suppose a color picker
doesn't really matter

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.873
when you're operating in gray scale.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.500
Each floor at Adobe is different.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.210
Some, like this one,
were more experimental,

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.310
tests to see what worked for employees.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.740
So now we're into this more
open office environment.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.110
- Yes, I think something
that's fascinating

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.290
about this floor is that
there are no offices.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.910
While everything, everything
in life is an experiment,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.680
we don't know exactly what's gonna work

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.470
better for you, that's gonna work for me.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:30.173
And so there is a spirit of innovation.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.990
- What we're trying to
do is be more innovative

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.760
and to do that you have
to experiment more.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.840
And you have almost create a culture

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.310
and an environment
where you can feel that.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.320
- [Adam] In a modular room called Lab 82,

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.330
named after the year Adobe was founded,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.690
the company tests ideas
in work place design

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:49.860
including how plants, sounds, colors,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.713
and scents can change a meeting.

00:03:52.754 --> 00:03:55.600
- ]Eric] And what we're
looking to do is identify

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.910
the best possible future ways
of being able to collaborate.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:01.530
- [Adam] And when something really worked,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.490
Engels and Adobe listened.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:04.910
These booths are really popular?

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.350
- Oh they're so popular that we had to

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.663
come back in and add this whole row.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.600
- [Adam] One of the
most noticable upgrades

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.620
Adobe made in the
redesign was the addition

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.210
of two new cafes.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.640
They went from one, to three.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.920
- This space is their
cafe called Pallettes.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.250
It's farm to fork concept and it really

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:30.320
harkens back to what this land was.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.410
You know this whole area was orchards.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.810
And so we really wanted
to give people a place

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.820
that recognized what the community was.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.700
Appreciate it; don't just
brush it under the rug.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.260
- [Adam] The cafe gets some of its produce

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.850
from the company run garden located

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:47.540
just outside its doors.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.440
- We're able to grow about
5000 pounds of produce

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.060
that we use in our cafes and
in our learning kitchens.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:54.850
And what's really great about it is,

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.150
we have employees come
and grow their own produce

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.100
here on site, we bring master
gardeners to teach them

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.800
different things that they can grow,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.450
and provide them seeds
that they can take home

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.140
and grow on their own.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.200
- [Adam] Adobe wants
employees to be healthy,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.990
and that means more than eating well.

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.430
In its wellness center,
employees have access to

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:15.930
cardio machines, weights,
classes, and this:

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.510
So Adobe has this thing
called the Soma Dome,

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.620
which is basically a guided
meditation experience

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.140
in this pink egg.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:25.223
I have to try it out.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.733
I'm getting ocean waves.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:31.763
It's relaxing.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:39.980
Adobe's redesign continues on some floors,

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.750
but across the street the company

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.360
is more than doubling its capacity

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.250
with a new tower set to open in 2022.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.760
Is the new office space going to look

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.910
like this space inside?

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.150
- There will be a lot of the essence

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.620
of what's happening here, but
also trying to think about

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.010
how people will be using
devices differently.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:58.840
How they'll be commuting differently.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.173
If you design perfect space for now,

00:06:02.134 --> 00:06:05.120
for a tower that's going to open in 2022,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.510
you can guarantee that
it's not going to work.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:10.260
- [Adam] Sounds like the
experimentation will continue.

